\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Pullbacks and the incidence map}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{Let #{R} be a commutative \vocabk{ring}{ca-000U} with identity, with the
\vocabk{carrier}{fdip-0010} of finite sets #{V,E} and maps #{s,h:E\to V},
and the \vocabk{field modules}{fdip-0010} over it. For a map #{a:E\to V}, its
\newvocab{field pullback} is
##{a^*:\mathcal F_R(V)\to\mathcal F_R(E),\qquad
   (a^*f)(e)=f(a(e)).}
It preserves addition, scalars, products and the unit. The
\newvocab{ring-valued incidence map} is the typed difference
##{\extd_{R}=h^*-s^*:\mathcal F_R(V)\longrightarrow\mathcal F_R(E).}
Thus #{(\extd_{R}f)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}. This incidence map is the
exterior derivative #{\covwedge} of \ref{fdip-00PV}. This is an
\vocabk{#{R}-linear map}{ca-001F}, with both coefficient rings equal to
#{R} and scalar homomorphism #{\sigma=\operatorname{id}_R}, as in
\ref{ca-001G}. The two field modules need not coincide, so #{\extd_{R}^2}
has no meaning without an additional composable map. The pullback law,
the linearity and the kernel of #{\extd_{R}} are in \ref{fdip-00AC}.}

\p{For real oriented-graph incidence, see
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}; the ring-level claims
follow from \ref{fdip-00AC}.}
